              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformations**\n\nI'm focusing on number grids and their transformations. I've observed a specific mapping in the training data: adjacent 2 and 3 values horizontally become 0 and 8. The input includes 0, 2, 3, 5, and 8, and the transformation seems related to the positions of 2s and 3s.\n\n\n**Refining Adjacency Rules**\n\nI've got a clearer picture of the 2-3 transformation: it's strictly about *orthogonal* adjacency. If 2 and 3 are next to each other, they become 0 and 8, no matter the order. I tested this by re-examining rows with 2s and 3s. Diagonally adjacent values are unchanged. Multiple adjacent pairs get the same transformation. I'm checking how this applies in more complex arrangements, looking at what happens when the 2s and 3s overlap or form patterns, and the case of multiple adjacent pairs.\n\n\n**Investigating Multivalency**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into how multiple adjacent 2s or 3s affect the transformation. It seems a single '2' can pair with multiple '3's, with all of them transforming. Specifically, if a '2' is orthogonally next to one or more '3's, the '2' becomes a '0', and each adjacent '3' becomes an '8'. I'm testing this new hypothesis.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
